---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import CtaBand from "../components/CtaBand.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
---

<BaseLayout title="Low MOQ label printing from 50 units" description="Low minimum label printing from 50 units per design: what the minimum covers, why converters quote in thousands, and how to keep a first run affordable.">
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Low MOQ label printing" }]} />
  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <h1>Low MOQ label printing</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.08rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
        Fifty units per design. That is the production minimum — enough to label a real first batch,
        test a market, or cover a short seasonal run without committing to thousands.
      </p>

      <h2>Why most label quotes start at 1,000</h2>
      <p>
        Traditional label printing is built around setup: plates, dies, make-ready and press time. The setup cost
        is roughly the same whether you print 500 labels or 50,000, so converters push the quantity up to spread it.
        Below their threshold the unit price becomes embarrassing, and they would rather not quote.
      </p>
      <p>
        Digital printing changes that arithmetic. There are no plates, the make-ready is short, and a 50-unit run
        is a legitimate job rather than a favour. That is the entire reason a 50-unit minimum is possible.
      </p>

      <h2>What 50 units actually covers</h2>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">First product batch</th><td>50–200 labels for a launch or a market test</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Multiple designs</th><td>Several designs combined in one order when material and size match</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Samples and photography</th><td>Enough for a shoot, a trade show or a buyer meeting</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Seasonal variants</th><td>A limited flavour or gift pack without leftover stock</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>How to keep a first run affordable</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Keep the shape simple.</strong> A circle or rounded rectangle avoids a custom die.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose a standard material.</strong> Coated paper or white PP costs less than speciality stocks.</li>
        <li><strong>Limit the number of designs.</strong> Each design adds setup, even in the same order.</li>
        <li><strong>Skip the laminate if the product stays dry.</strong> It is a durability decision, not a default.</li>
        <li><strong>Reuse the artwork on reorder.</strong> The approved proof carries over, so the second run is faster and cheaper.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What low MOQ does not mean</h2>
      <p>
        It does not mean a low price per unit. At 50 units, most of what you pay is setup and make-ready, so the
        unit price is two to four times what it would be at 5,000. The honest comparison is not "labels cost $0.80 each"
        but "the first 50 labels cost more per unit, and that is the price of not holding 5,000 in a cupboard".
      </p>
      <p>
        It also does not mean every material is available. Some stocks and finishes only make sense in larger runs;
        we will say so rather than quote something that cannot be produced properly at your quantity.
      </p>

      <h2>When to move up in quantity</h2>
      <p>
        Once a design is selling steadily, the per-unit price at 500 or 1,000 is materially lower and the reorder
        skips proofing. The usual path is 50 to validate, 250–500 to cover the next few months, then 1,000+ on a
        roll once volume is predictable.
      </p>

      <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/request-a-quote/">Request a quote for 50+ labels</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <CtaBand subject="Low MOQ label quotation" body={`Tell us the size, material, quantity and destination. Minimum ${site.moq} units.`} />
</BaseLayout>
